[CommunityReport(id='100', short_id='100', title='Norman Conquest and Its Historical Artifacts', community_id='100', summary='This community centers around significant historical artifacts and figures related to the Norman Conquest of England, including the Bayeux Tapestry, the Domesday Book, and William the Conqueror. These entities are interconnected through their roles in documenting and commemorating the events of the conquest, reflecting the socio-political changes that ensued.', full_content="# Norman Conquest and Its Historical Artifacts\n\nThis community centers around significant historical artifacts and figures related to the Norman Conquest of England, including the Bayeux Tapestry, the Domesday Book, and William the Conqueror. These entities are interconnected through their roles in documenting and commemorating the events of the conquest, reflecting the socio-political changes that ensued.\n\n## Bayeux Tapestry as a historical narrative\n\nThe Bayeux Tapestry is a crucial artifact that visually narrates the events leading up to the Norman Conquest, particularly the Battle of Hastings. This embroidered cloth serves not only as a historical record but also as a piece of propaganda that celebrates the Norman victory. Its intricate designs provide insights into the perspectives of the Normans during this pivotal time, making it an essential resource for understanding the cultural and historical context of the conquest. The tapestry's relationship with the Norman Conquest is underscored by its depiction of key events and figures involved in the invasion [Data: Entities (519); Relationships (1997, 1260)].\n\n## Domesday Book's role in governance\n\nCommissioned by William the Conqueror in 1085, the Domesday Book is a comprehensive survey that documents landholdings in England for taxation purposes. This significant record reflects the social and economic structure of the time, highlighting the changes in governance that occurred following the Norman Conquest.